WEBVTT

00:00:00.700 --> 00:00:03.220
Here is a realistic, disciplined trading plan.

00:00:03.770 --> 00:00:09.380
A $1,000,000 balance. Three trades a day, three days a week. One percent risk on each.

00:00:11.070 --> 00:00:14.680
Each trade risks $10,000. That is the stop loss.

00:00:15.230 --> 00:00:20.440
The target is 3 percent: $30,000. A risk-to-reward of one to three.

00:00:20.990 --> 00:00:25.270
And once a trade moves into profit, the stop loss moves to break-even.

00:00:26.970 --> 00:00:32.080
Watch one trade. Entry here. Stop loss below. Target three times further above.

00:00:32.630 --> 00:00:37.500
The price moves up, into profit. Now the stop loss moves up to the entry price.

00:00:38.050 --> 00:00:42.620
From here, the trade cannot lose. If the price turns back, it closes at zero.

00:00:43.170 --> 00:00:46.920
If it keeps going, it hits the target: plus $30,000.

00:00:48.970 --> 00:00:53.020
A realistic day. One trade hits the target: plus $30,000.

00:00:53.570 --> 00:00:55.900
One returns to break-even: zero.

00:00:56.450 --> 00:00:59.310
One hits the stop loss: minus $10,000.

00:00:59.860 --> 00:01:02.770
The day: plus $20,000. Two percent.

00:01:04.500 --> 00:01:08.020
At one to three, you break even winning just one trade in four.

00:01:08.570 --> 00:01:13.950
Even the worst day, three losses, costs $30,000. Three percent of the account.

00:01:15.630 --> 00:01:18.590
Three days a week is about 13 trading days a month.

00:01:19.140 --> 00:01:23.980
If the average day matched the example: $260,000 a month.

00:01:24.530 --> 00:01:29.480
Month by month, the balance climbs to about $4.1 million after twelve months.

00:01:31.170 --> 00:01:33.780
Now withdraw 3 percent of the balance every month.

00:01:34.330 --> 00:01:39.190
Your initial $1,000,000 is fully withdrawn by month 14, while the account keeps growing.

00:01:43.170 --> 00:01:46.390
These are projections from one example day, not results.

00:01:46.940 --> 00:01:55.980
